this is a production of Cornell  University  hi this Frank Rossi i'm at the bethpage  state park here in late april of 2011  and i'm looking over the greens here and  noticing one of the first things I'm  noticing is the sort of yellowing that  we get from that early proxy primo  application you look real closely at the  putting surfaces and you notice that you  really see the dramatic differences  between the bent grass and the annual  bluegrass the annual bluegrass that sort  of flash of yellowing that you get from  the proxy application for seed has  depression and then at this time of year  it's very common that the bank grass  will just be very slow to go it's much  late starter and so you'll see the real  differentiation between the two growth  habits at this point the other thing you  notice at the springtime that I've  noticed consistently is where we've done  the sodding work around bunkers are  around green surrounds to replace the  blue grass sod we've consistently  replaced it with sort of low grow tight  Kentucky bluegrass or the elite types of  Kentucky bluegrass that that we use in  sod production and one of the things we  notice about that is that it's very slow  to green up in the springtime and so you  can really get a good example of sort of  why if you need really good spring  quality turf why some of these low grow  elite varieties may not be the most  ideal for most situations we're looking  forward to a great growing season here  at bethpage and hope you follow along  with us all year this has been a  production of Cornell University on the  web at cornell.edu
 

